  "preamble": "Raising of the near-open front vowel [æ] to the close-mid front vowel [e]. It is best documented in the Old English dialects, where Anglian raised the long vowel ǣ to ē (West Saxon dǣd, Anglian dēd 'deed') and the Mercian 'Second Fronting' raised short æ to e (dæġ > deġ 'day'), and in Persian, where the word-final front vowel of Early New Persian was raised to [e] (xāna > xâne 'house').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Raising, Vowel Fronting",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Old English (Anglian)",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "West Saxon dǣd 'deed' (< *dādiz)",
          "to": "Anglian dēd",
          "note": "Post-brightening ǣ was raised to ē in Anglian but kept in West Saxon; also West Saxon slǣpan vs Anglian slēpan 'to sleep'."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Old English (Mercian, Vespasian Psalter)",
      "languageFamily": "West Germanic",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "dæġ [dæj] 'day' (< Proto-Germanic *dagaz)",
          "to": "deġ",
          "note": "Second Fronting, confined to part of the Mercian area, fronted /a/ to /æ/ and raised /æ/ to /e/; deġ is the Vespasian Psalter spelling."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Persian",
      "languageFamily": "Iranian",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Classical Persian xāna /xaː.ˈna/ 'house' (final vowel fronted to [æ])",
          "to": "Iranian Persian xâne /xɒː.ˈne/",
          "note": "Word-final /æ/ of Early New Persian mostly shifted to /e/ in contemporary Iranian Persian; Dari and Tajik keep final -a (xāna, xona)."
        }
      ]
    },
